<meta type=article>
  <title | Margin notes>
  <author | Enscribe>
</meta>

<section | Notes in the page margin>

A margin note is a note positioned in the page margin — `<note position=margin>`.<note position=margin | A margin note is numbered and collected like any note; its content is also projected into the margin beside its marker.> It renders like any other note: one note type, three positions (foot, end, margin).

The body of a margin note takes normal inline interpretation.<note position=margin | Inline markup works here: *emphasis*, `code`, and other inline elements all interpret normally.> So emphasis, code, citations, and cross-references all work inside it.

Position is a per-note choice, independent of the document `note-position` default. A footnote collects to the foot of the document instead.<note placement=foot | This is an ordinary footnote — position=foot, numbered and collected at the foot of the document.> The two differ only in position; both are numbered notes.

<section | On narrow screens>

Below the margin breakpoint (a narrow window) there is no margin to float into, so the projected margin copy hides and the note shows in the bottom note-list instead.<note position=margin | Resize the window narrow to see this margin note fall back to the bottom list.> The text stays readable either way.
